   "validatorKey": "y10_1_3_l2_do_now",
   "content": "<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Do Now: Unit Conversions</h2><p class=\"lesson-lead\">Recapping 1.3 L1. Cambridge IGCSE 0478/12, March 2023, Question 3(a): data storage is measured using binary denominations. Complete each conversion.</p><div class=\"lesson-exam-card\"><div class=\"lesson-do-now-responses\"><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y10-13l2-do-now-a\">(a) 8 bytes = ................ nibbles</label><input id=\"y10-13l2-do-now-a\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-kind=\"short\" data-answer-id=\"do-now-a\" aria-label=\"Do Now: Unit Conversions part a\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y10-13l2-do-now-b\">(b) 512 kibibytes (KiB) = ................ mebibytes (MiB)</label><input id=\"y10-13l2-do-now-b\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-kind=\"short\" data-answer-id=\"do-now-b\" aria-label=\"Do Now: Unit Conversions part b\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y10-13l2-do-now-c\">(c) 4 gibibytes (GiB) = ................ mebibytes (MiB)</label><input id=\"y10-13l2-do-now-c\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-kind=\"short\" data-answer-id=\"do-now-c\" aria-label=\"Do Now: Unit Conversions part c\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-response\"><label for=\"y10-13l2-do-now-d\">(d) 1 exbibyte (EiB) = ................ pebibytes (PiB)</label><input id=\"y10-13l2-do-now-d\" class=\"pseudocode-output-input lesson-exam-answer\" data-answer-kind=\"short\" data-answer-id=\"do-now-d\" aria-label=\"Do Now: Unit Conversions part d\" autocomplete=\"off\"></div></div><div class=\"lesson-do-now-actions\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"donow-btn\" id=\"y10-13l2-do-now-check\">Check answers</button><strong>Total: 4 marks</strong></div><div id=\"y10-13l2-do-now-feedback\" class=\"pseudocode-feedback\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div></div>"
  },
  {
   "id": "do-now-2",
   "label": "Do Now: The Unit Ladder",
   "type": "multiple-choice",
   "containerId": "y10-13l2-do-now-2-mcq",
   "content": "<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Do Now: The Unit Ladder</h2><p class=\"lesson-lead\">Recapping 1.3 L1. Based on Cambridge IGCSE 0478/12, March 2026, Question 1(a) and 0478/12, June 2024, Question 1(c).</p><div id=\"y10-13l2-do-now-2-mcq\"></div>",
   "items": [
    {
     "prompt": "Which statement about data storage is correct?",
     "options": [
      "One bit equals four nibbles",
      "One byte equals 16 bits",
      "One exbibyte (EiB) equals 1024 x 1024 tebibytes (TiB)",
      "One mebibyte (MiB) equals 1024 x 1024 gibibytes (GiB)"
     ],
     "correct": 2,
     "explain": "TiB to PiB is x1024, then PiB to EiB is x1024 again. The other three have the ladder the wrong way round or the wrong numbers."
    },
    {
     "prompt": "Give the name of the data storage measurement that is equal to 1024 gibibytes (GiB).",
     "options": [
      "Mebibyte (MiB)",
      "Exbibyte (EiB)",
      "Pebibyte (PiB)",
      "Tebibyte (TiB)"
     ],
     "correct": 3,
     "explain": "The ladder goes GiB, TiB, PiB, EiB. 1024 GiB is one step up: 1 TiB."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "title",
   "label": "File Size Calculations",
   "content": "<div class=\"lesson-title-slide\"><p class=\"lesson-title-kicker\">Data Storage and Compression</p><h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">File Size Calculations</h2><p>Year 10, 1.3 L2 &middot; Image and Sound Files &middot; 30 minutes</p></div><ul class=\"lesson-facts\"><li><strong>Concept:</strong> The image and sound file size formulas, finished in the unit the question asks for.</li><li><strong>Walkthroughs:</strong> One image and one sound calculation, step by step.</li><li><strong>Challenge:</strong> Storage Blitz, the Images and Sound levels.</li><li><strong>Exam link:</strong> Two real Cambridge image calculations.</li></ul>"
  },
  {
   "id": "concept-image",
   "label": "Image File Size",
   "content": "<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Image File Size</h2><p class=\"lesson-lead\">An image file stores a colour for every pixel, so its size depends on how many pixels there are and how many bits each one needs.</p><ul class=\"lesson-facts\"><li><strong>File size in bits</strong> = width &times; height &times; colour depth (bits)</li><li>Divide by 8 for bytes, then by 1024 for KiB, then by 1024 again for MiB.</li><li>If the colour depth is given in <strong>bytes</strong>, width &times; height &times; bytes already gives bytes. Do not divide by 8.</li><li>Stop at the unit the question asks for, and show your working.</li></ul>"
  },
  {
   "id": "worked-image",
   "label": "Worked Example: Image to KiB",
   "type": "stepper",
   "stepperId": "y10-13l2-worked-image-stepper",
   "stepperKey": "y10_image_file_size",
   "content": "<h2 class=\"lesson-h2\">Worked Example: Image to KiB</h2><p class=\"lesson-lead\">Step through one full image calculation, from pixels to kibibytes.</p><div id=\"y10-13l2-worked-image-stepper\" class=\"lesson-stepper\"></div>"
  },
  {
